  "worked_solution": "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=1.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =-7{c}_{1}{x}^{-8}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-8}-7{c}_{2}{x}^{-8}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-7(1)+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={x}^{-7}+8{x}^{-7}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x \\\\  & =\\frac{1+8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{{x}^{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
